# November 23, 2026: a first scatter of places across Quebec outside the Mauricie and the Île d'Orléans, which the two earlier
# Quebec batches covered: Saguenay–Lac-Saint-Jean, Abitibi-Témiscamingue, Outaouais, Laurentides, Montreal, Montérégie,
# Estrie, Chaudière-Appalaches, Bas-Saint-Laurent, Gaspésie and Côte-Nord. Region qc, taken by tools/choose.py --thinnest
# (Quebec then had 36 places). Sources opened in this session: the Canadian Register of Historic Places statement for each
# place (historicplaces.ca, read with tools/page.py --all; the Quebec-listed ones in French, from the Ministère de la Culture);
# the Wikipedia "List of historic places in <region>" pages (raw text) for Register ids and coordinates; the English Wikipedia
# articles on Val-Jalbert, Fort Lennox and Fort Témiscamingue; and the operators' own pages for hours: Parks Canada (Fort
# Chambly, Coteau-du-Lac, Fort Lennox, Manoir Papineau, Carillon Canal, Obadjiwan–Fort Témiscamingue, Pointe-au-Père,
# Grosse-Île), Château Ramezay, Parc historique de la Pointe-du-Moulin, Fort Ingall, La Cité de l'Or, La Pulperie (archived
# copy of March 9, 2026) and the City of Montreal (Moulin Fleming). Nothing was seen on the ground.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HP(t,i): return ['Canadian Register of Historic Places: '+t,'https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=%d'%i]
def PC(t,s): return ['Parks Canada: '+t,'https://parcs.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/qc/'+s]

BATCH={'key':'qc_qftj','label':'Across Quebec: first places in eleven regions','added':'2026-11-23 02:34'}

PLACES=[
N('val-jalbert',"Village historique de Val-Jalbert",'Chambord','qc','town','standing',48.4379,-72.1664,'site','open','walk','','1901–1927',
  "A company town built from 1901 round a mechanical wood-pulp mill at the foot of the Ouiatchouan Falls, which drop nearly 72 m into Lac Saint-Jean country. Damase Jalbert founded the Ouiatchouan pulp company in 1901; the Compagnie de pulpe de Chicoutimi took it over in 1909, one of the few pulp businesses run by French Canadians. The mill shut abruptly in 1927 and the village emptied (Wikipedia; the Register says in the 1930s). The province took it over as a tourist site in 1960, and it was classified a historic site in 1996.",
  "By the Register the protected ground holds 94 buildings along the river: the stone pulp mill with its boiler, turbines, grinder, screens and hydraulic presses still inside, the wooden workers' houses built to four standard plans in an upper residential town, the general store and hotel, the butcher's, the wooden convent school, the foundations of the church of Saint-Georges-de-la-Ouiatchouan, the remains of the presbytery, the first chapel, the manager's house, a stable and a flour mill, and the remains of the dam, the penstock and the railway landing. Wikipedia counts over 70 original abandoned buildings. The operator's page shows a cable car up to lookouts over the falls.",
  "Canada's best-kept ghost town: a 1920s pulp village emptied overnight, with its mill machinery still in place.",
  "Run as a heritage village and tourist site at 95 rue Saint-Georges, Chambord (the Register names the Sépaq and the MRC Le Domaine-du-Roy as past managers). Entry is by passport sold by the operator; its hours page could not be read when this was written (hours not checked). Mapped lot on rue Saint-Georges about 600 m north-east of the pin (OpenStreetMap; not checked on the ground).",
  [HP("Village historique de Val-Jalbert",5503),W('Val-Jalbert'),L('Village historique de Val-Jalbert','https://www.valjalbert.com/'),W('List of historic places in Saguenay-Lac-Saint-Jean')],
  th=[48.4417,-72.16059],thn="Lot on rue Saint-Georges (OpenStreetMap)",brick='?',hours='?'),

N('pulperie-de-chicoutimi',"La Pulperie de Chicoutimi (Old Chicoutimi Pulp Mill)",'Saguenay (Chicoutimi)','qc','industry','standing',48.42253,-71.08324,'exact','open','walk','','1896–1930',
  "The main works of the Compagnie de pulpe de Chicoutimi, founded in 1896 by the mayor of Chicoutimi, Joseph-Dominique Guay, with partners including J.-É.-A. Dubuc. Around 1910 it was the largest maker of mechanical wood pulp in Canada, sold for newsprint. The mill closed in 1930, but most of its buildings still stand. A national historic site since 1983, it is now the regional museum.",
  "Five stone mill buildings of 1898 to 1923 in a partly wooded valley on the Chicoutimi River, all with many windows: Mill 1 (1898, metal mansard roof), Mill 2 (1903, arched openings and a curved Queen Anne style gable, with some of its working fittings inside), Mill 3 (1912, set on two arms of the river), Mill 4 (1921, repair shop, with mezzanines and overhead cranes inside) and the cut-stone electrical substation (1912–1913, enlarged 1923). The museum's exhibitions are inside; the grounds are a large park (operator's page).",
  "A whole stone pulp-mill complex of 1898 to 1923, standing in a river gorge in the middle of Chicoutimi.",
  "Museum run by La Pulperie de Chicoutimi, 300 rue Dubuc. The hours below are from the museum's page in its archived copy of March 9, 2026 (the live page would not load when this was written); summer hours were not read. Mapped lot on rue Dubuc about 350 m east of the museum building (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Old Chicoutimi Pulp Mill National Historic Site of Canada",15903),L('La Pulperie de Chicoutimi','https://www.pulperie.com/en/'),W('List of historic places in Saguenay-Lac-Saint-Jean')],
  th=[48.42223,-71.0786],thn="Lot on rue Dubuc (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no',
  hours='March 9 to June 16, 2026: Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. (archived page; summer not read)'),

N('fort-chambly',"Fort Chambly",'Chambly','qc','fort','standing',45.44971,-73.27696,'exact','open','walk','','1709–1711',
  "A square stone fort with four corner bastions at the foot of the Chambly rapids on the Richelieu River. Captain Jacques de Chambly built the first wooden fort here in 1665 against the Iroquois; the present stone fort, the fourth on the site, went up in 1709 to 1711 against the British. Ceded to Britain in 1760, held by the Americans in 1775, used in the War of 1812 and the rebellions of 1837 and 1838, it was abandoned in the mid-1800s. A townsman, Joseph-Octave Dion, looked after it from 1875 to 1916; Parks Canada restored it in 1983.",
  "High stone curtain walls with bartizans, musket loopholes and embrasures, round a courtyard closed by stone buildings set against the walls; the entrance gateway is rusticated stone carved with names from New France, under a gallery and a pediment with a coat of arms. A Palladian guardhouse, the soldiers' burial ground as a plain lawn, a stretch of the old Chambly road and archaeological traces of the first village (1672–1711) are part of the site. The buildings hold a small museum (Register).",
  "The French stone fort of 1711 on the Richelieu rapids, rescued by one man and then restored.",
  "Parks Canada national historic site, 2 rue Richelieu, Chambly. The fort opens spring to autumn, and the waterside park round it is open all year (Parks Canada hours page, read November 2026; closed for the season until spring 2027). Mapped lots P1 and P2 on rue du Fort (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Fort Chambly National Historic Site of Canada",10175),W('Fort Chambly'),PC('Fort Chambly hours','fortchambly/visit/heures-hours'),W('List of historic places in Montérégie')],
  th=[45.44864,-73.27457],thn="Lot P2, rue du Fort (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no',
  hours='2026: May 16 to June 14 and September 8 to October 12, Wednesday to Sunday; June 15 to September 7 daily; 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),

N('coteau-du-lac',"Coteau-du-Lac canal and fort",'Coteau-du-Lac','qc','canal','ruins',45.2878,-74.1766,'site','public','walk','','1779–1856',
  "Remains of what the Register calls the first lock canal in North America, cut in 1779 to 1781 past the Coteau-du-Lac rapids of the St. Lawrence and doubled in width in 1814 to 1817 for Durham boats. From 1778 to the mid-1800s a British military post guarded the river route to Upper Canada here, and from 1797 to 1840 a customs office made it the main port of entry into Upper Canada. A dyke and dam built upstream in the 1940s dropped the water about 2.5 m.",
  "On a point facing the rapids: the cut of the old canal and its drain canal, earthworks, a clover-shaped battery, the remains of many military buildings, a replica of the 1813 octagonal blockhouse, and archaeological traces from 1779 to 1856 (Register). The outdoor site is self-guided.",
  "One of the oldest lock canals in North America, with the earthworks of the fort that guarded it.",
  "Parks Canada national historic site, 308A chemin du Fleuve. The outdoor site can be visited on your own all year; the visitor centre and toilets open only during the summer programmes run by the Parc régional du canal de Soulanges (Wednesdays, July 8 to August 19, 2026, 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., free). There is a parking lot and a bike rack (Parks Canada hours page, read November 2026). Mapped lot on rue du Fort about 200 m north-west (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Coteau-du-Lac National Historic Site of Canada",11675),PC('Coteau-du-Lac hours','coteaudulac/visit/heures-hours'),W('List of historic places in Montérégie')],
  th=[45.28852,-74.17894],thn="Lot on rue du Fort (OpenStreetMap)",brick='?',
  hours='Outdoor site open all year, self-guided'),

N('fort-lennox',"Fort Lennox",'Saint-Paul-de-l\'Île-aux-Noix','qc','fort','standing',45.12101,-73.26975,'site','boat','boat','','1819–1870',
  "A British fort that covers most of Île aux Noix in the Richelieu River, a few kilometres from the United States border. The French first fortified the island in 1759; the British took it in 1760, American forces used it in 1775 and 1776, and the British refortified it from 1778. The present fort, the third on the island, was built in 1819 to 1828 and garrisoned until 1870; the militia trained there until 1921, and from 1940 to 1943 it held a camp for European Jewish refugees. A national historic site since 1920.",
  "Stone barracks, officers' quarters, guardhouse and magazines in a classical style inside ramparts and a star-shaped water-filled moat, with the parade ground, glacis, trenches and cemeteries round them (Register). Wikipedia says the north and south magazines are closed to visitors.",
  "A moated stone fort of the 1820s filling an island in the Richelieu.",
  "Parks Canada national historic site, reached by a paid ferry to Île aux Noix from 1 61e Avenue, Saint-Paul-de-l'Île-aux-Noix; entry to the fort itself is free; mapped lot at the end of 61e Avenue by the ferry landing (OpenStreetMap). Parks Canada's hours page says the fort is closed for the whole 2026 season because of damp and air quality in some buildings, and normally opens in summer (read November 2026).",
  [HP("Fort Lennox National Historic Site of Canada",12608),W('Fort Lennox'),PC('Fort Lennox hours','lennox/visit/heures-hours'),W('List of historic places in Montérégie')],
  th=[45.12688,-73.27209],thn="Lot on 61e Avenue by the ferry (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no',hours='Closed for the 2026 season (Parks Canada); usually open in summer'),

N('moulin-pointe-du-moulin-ile-perrot',"Île Perrot windmill and miller's house (Pointe-du-Moulin)",'Notre-Dame-de-l\'Île-Perrot','qc','industry','standing',45.3659,-73.8521,'site','open','walk','','1707–1791',
  "A stone tower windmill built in 1707 and 1708 for Joseph Trottier Desruisseaux, designed by the master mason Jean Mars and the master carpenter Léonard Paillé dit Paillard, so that local farmers could grind wheat. The miller's house beside it was begun in 1786. A windmill still paired with its miller's house is now very rare, which is why the two were named a national historic site in 1969.",
  "On the eastern point of Île Perrot over Lac Saint-Louis: a cylindrical rubblestone mill of three and a half storeys under a conical cap with triangular dormers, with sails and machinery on the tower and wood sheathing inside, and a one-and-a-half-storey rubblestone house under a hipped roof with low doors, small windows and old panelled doors, shutters and floors (Register). The park's guides in period dress show the mill and the house, which has an interpretation centre (park's page).",
  "A 1707 stone windmill still standing beside its miller's house on a point in Lac Saint-Louis.",
  "Parc historique de la Pointe-du-Moulin, 2500 boulevard Don-Quichotte; admission charged (tickets online). The park's page says the grounds can be walked all year, but with no staff out of season. Mapped lot on boulevard Don-Quichotte about 400 m west (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Île Perrot Windmill and Miller's House National Historic Site of Canada",14506),L('Parc historique de la Pointe-du-Moulin: hours','https://pointedumoulin.com/?p=2215'),W('List of historic places in Montérégie')],
  th=[45.36683,-73.85724],thn="Lot on boulevard Don-Quichotte (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no',
  hours='May 16 to October 12, 2026: Saturdays, Sundays and holidays 9:30 a.m. to 5 p.m.; daily June 22 to August 22'),

N('chateau-ramezay',"Château Ramezay",'Montreal (Old Montreal)','qc','landmark','standing',45.50876,-73.55317,'exact','open','walk','','1705',
  "A stone mansion built in 1705 for Claude de Ramezay, governor of Montreal from 1704 to 1724. The Compagnie des Indes occidentales used it from 1745 to 1763 and rebuilt it after a fire in 1756; from 1773 to 1844 it was the residence of the governors of British North America, except in 1775 and 1776 when American commanders lived there. Later it held government offices, courts and schools, and since 1895 it has been the museum of the Antiquarian and Numismatic Society of Montreal.",
  "A one-and-a-half-storey stone house behind a low wall on rue Notre-Dame Est, with stepped gable parapets, cut-stone trim and tall stone chimneys, an east wing with a conical-roofed tower added in 1903, vaulted basements inside, and the Governor's Garden laid out behind it in 2000 (Register). The museum is inside.",
  "The French governor's house of 1705 where American commanders lived during the 1775 invasion.",
  "Château Ramezay museum, 280 rue Notre-Dame Est. Adults $14.99 with tax (museum's page, read November 2026). Mapped lot on rue Le Royer Est about 70 m south-east (OpenStreetMap; likely paid).",
  [HP("Château De Ramezay / India House National Historic Site of Canada",15825),W('Château Ramezay'),L('Château Ramezay: information','https://www.chateauramezay.qc.ca/en/information/'),W('List of historic places in Montreal')],
  th=[45.50824,-73.55292],thn="Lot on rue Le Royer Est (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no',
  hours='Museum daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Governor\'s Garden daily 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.'),

N('moulin-fleming-lasalle',"Moulin Fleming",'Montreal (LaSalle)','qc','industry','standing',45.42606,-73.66016,'exact','public','walk','','1827',
  "A stone tower windmill built in 1827 by the mason William Morrison for William Fleming, a Scottish immigrant who had already put up a mill in 1815 against the seigneurial rules and fought the Sulpicians, seigneurs of Montreal Island, in court from 1816 until they dropped the case in 1825. It is the only British-type tower mill in Quebec. It fell out of use about 1891, was patched up in 1930, bought by the town of LaSalle in 1947, classified in 1983, and restored in 1990 and again in 2024 after high winds (City of Montreal page; Register).",
  "A five-storey truncated cone of thick stone, wide at the base, under a domed cap with four long sails on one side and a fantail wheel on the other; traces of the gallery that ringed the tower and of the old board cladding show on the walls. The inside machinery is gone (Register).",
  "Quebec's one British-style tower windmill, the fruit of a decade-long fight with the seigneurs.",
  "City of Montreal property, 9675 boulevard LaSalle, in a park by the St. Lawrence, with free parking, picnic tables and a bike rack (City of Montreal page). It is seen from the park; the city's page gives no visiting hours for the inside (interior not checked).",
  [HP("Moulin à vent Fleming",6954),L('Ville de Montréal: Moulin Fleming','https://montreal.ca/lieux/moulin-fleming'),W('List of historic places in Montreal')],
  th=[45.42619,-73.6597],thn="Free lot by the mill (City of Montreal; OpenStreetMap)",brick='no'),

N('manoir-papineau',"Manoir Papineau",'Montebello','qc','landmark','standing',45.64611,-74.9458,'site','open','walk','','1846–1855',
  "The country house of Louis-Joseph Papineau, the leader of the Patriotes, built on his seigneury of the Petite-Nation after he left politics in 1837; he moved in in 1846, and most of the estate's buildings date from before his death in 1855. His family lived there until 1929; it then passed to an investment company that became the Seigniory Club (1933) and to Canadian Pacific (1949). Parks Canada has restored it and opened it to visitors.",
  "A four-storey house with corner towers, mixed roofs and a classical five-bay front on a bluff over the Ottawa River, with fine woodwork inside and out, wrought-iron balconies, and a square library tower with an iron roof and shutters against fire. On the grounds: the red-brick grain storehouse with Napoléon Bourassa's workshop and its frescoed murals, a tea house, a family chapel, a dam on the Papineau stream, and the remains of an ice house and fountains (Register). The inside of the manor is seen only on guided tours (Parks Canada).",
  "The Patriote leader's riverside manor, with its fireproof library tower.",
  "Parks Canada national historic site beside the Château Montebello hotel, Montebello. Guided tours of the manor in French at 11:15 a.m., 2 p.m. and 4:15 p.m. and in English at 10:45 a.m., 1:30 p.m. and 3:15 p.m.; exhibitions in the other buildings are open during opening hours (Parks Canada hours page, read November 2026; closed for the season until spring 2027). Mapped Manoir Papineau lot about 400 m north-east of the pin (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Manoir Papineau National Historic Site of Canada",7639),PC('Manoir Papineau hours','manoirpapineau/visit/heures-hours'),W('List of historic places in Outaouais')],
  th=[45.64969,-74.94378],thn="Manoir Papineau lot (OpenStreetMap)",brick='some',
  hours='2026: May 16 to June 23 and September 8 to October 12 weekends; June 24 to September 7 Wednesday to Sunday; 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),

N('canal-de-carillon',"Carillon Canal",'Saint-André-d\'Argenteuil (Carillon)','qc','canal','standing',45.5684,-74.378,'site','public','walk','','1829–1963',
  "A canal round the Long-Sault rapids of the Ottawa River, built in 1829 to 1833 by the Royal Staff Corps of the British Army as a military and immigrant route between Montreal and Kingston by the Ottawa and Rideau rivers. It was enlarged for trade in 1873 to 1882 and largely remade when Hydro-Québec built its dam here in 1960 to 1963. It now carries pleasure boats through a single lock with a guillotine gate that lifts them about 20 m (Parks Canada).",
  "The combined locks at the lower river entrance of the first canal, the south stone wall of the second canal along the present channel, the traces of the old feeder canal, and the superintendent's house and the stone toll collector's house beside the entrance (Commons photographs show the toll house with a red-brick chimney); the Carillon Barracks stand nearby (Register). The modern lock works in season.",
  "A British military canal of 1833 beside a 20-metre guillotine lock on the Ottawa.",
  "Parks Canada national historic site, 210 rue du Barrage, Carillon. Open daily from sunrise to 11 p.m.; the lock runs from 9:30 a.m. in the boating season (Parks Canada hours page, read November 2026). Mapped lot on route du Long-Sault about 260 m south-east (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Carillon Canal National Historic Site of Canada",11676),PC('Carillon Canal hours','canalcarillon/visit/heures-hours'),W('Carillon Canal'),W('List of historic places in Laurentides')],
  th=[45.56679,-74.37559],thn="Lot on route du Long-Sault (OpenStreetMap)",brick='some',
  hours='Daily, sunrise to 11 p.m.'),

N('moulin-legare',"Moulin Légaré",'Saint-Eustache','qc','industry','standing',45.5581,-73.8962,'site','open','walk','','1762–1763',
  "A water-powered flour mill built in 1762 and 1763 by François Maisonneuve on land the seigneur of Mille-Îles granted him on condition that he build a mill, on the Rivière du Chêne at the edge of the old village of Saint-Eustache. The Register says it has worked continuously since the French Regime, over 240 years. The name comes from the Légaré family, who owned it in the 1900s.",
  "Opposite the town hall on rue Saint-Eustache: the low flour mill of 1762–1763 with wood siding, a cut-stone foundation, dormers, small windows and the miller's old living quarters inside, with remains of its water wheel and later turbines; a wood-frame sawmill of 1880 joined to it; the miller's house of 1902–1903 with a low mansard roof and its original woodwork inside; and the dam and millpond of 1762–1763 (Register).",
  "A mill that has ground flour on the same stream since 1763.",
  "232 rue Saint-Eustache, run by a non-profit corporation. Its own website could not be opened when this was written (hours not checked). Mapped lot on rue Saint-Eustache about 150 m north-west (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Légaré Mill National Historic Site of Canada",12429),W('List of historic places in Laurentides')],
  th=[45.55893,-73.89771],thn="Lot on rue Saint-Eustache (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no',hours='?'),

N('phare-pointe-au-pere',"Pointe-au-Père Lighthouse",'Rimouski (Pointe-au-Père)','qc','light','standing',48.51746,-68.46902,'exact','open','walk','','1859–1997',
  "A light station on a headland where the St. Lawrence opens to the sea, first lit in 1859; the present 24 m concrete tower, the third, dates from 1909. For about a century it was the base of the St. Lawrence pilots; it also kept a tide and current station (1894–1985), took on some of the Grosse-Île quarantine work (1923–1937), and tested fog signals: the diaphone tried here became the standard in most Canadian lighthouses in 1904. Fisheries and Oceans closed the station in 1997.",
  "Red-roofed white buildings round the tall concrete tower: the keeper's and assistant keeper's houses, the fog-alarm building, a garage, the cistern and the generator, with presumed traces of the first two lighthouses (Register). Visitors can go in the lighthouse, the keeper's house, the assistant keeper's house and the foghorn shed (Parks Canada).",
  "The station where Canada's standard foghorn was tried out, with its 1909 concrete tower open to climb.",
  "Parks Canada national historic site, 1034 rue du Phare, part of the Pointe-au-Père Maritime Historic Site. The ticket office closes at 4 p.m. (Parks Canada hours page, read November 2026; only the late-season dates were shown). Mapped lot on rue du Phare about 70 m east (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Pointe-au-Père Lighthouse National Historic Site of Canada",9912),W('Pointe-au-Père Lighthouse'),PC('Pointe-au-Père hours','pointeaupere/visit/heures-hours'),W('List of historic places in Bas-Saint-Laurent')],
  th=[48.51725,-68.46785],thn="Lot on rue du Phare (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no',
  hours='August 17 to October 12, 2026: 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. (earlier summer dates not shown)'),

N('fort-ingall',"Fort Ingall",'Témiscouata-sur-le-Lac (Cabano)','qc','fort','standing',47.6911,-68.901,'site','open','walk','','1839–1842',
  "A British log fort built in 1839 to 1841 under Lieutenant Lennox Ingall on a terrace over Lac Témiscouata, during the Aroostook War, the border quarrel between New Brunswick and Maine. With Fort Dégelé and the posts at Petit-Sault and Grand-Sault it was to keep the Americans from the St. Lawrence and guard the Témiscouata portage on the overland road from Quebec to Halifax. The quarrel ended with the Webster–Ashburton treaty of August 9, 1842, without a shot fired. The fort was rebuilt on its dug-up footings in the 1970s.",
  "A wooden palisade enclosing six rebuilt log buildings: the north and south barracks, the officers' quarters, the guardhouse, the commissariat and the latrines. Digs found the original barracks, guardhouse, powder magazine, bakery and kitchen, a lime kiln and two wells (Register).",
  "A log fort of the bloodless Aroostook War, rebuilt on its own footings over Lac Témiscouata.",
  "Fort Ingall museum, 81 rue Caldwell. Open from Victoria Day (Journée nationale des Patriotes) to Thanksgiving; adults $15 in the low season; a full visit takes about an hour and a half (fort's schedule page, read November 2026). Mapped lot on rue Caldwell about 100 m south (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Fort Ingall",11987),W('Fort Ingall'),L('Fort Ingall: schedule and rates','https://fortingall.ca/en/schedule-and-rates/'),W('List of historic places in Bas-Saint-Laurent')],
  th=[47.6903,-68.90053],thn="Lot on rue Caldwell (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no',
  hours='June 24 to Labour Day daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Victoria Day to June 23 and Labour Day to Thanksgiving Wednesday to Sunday 9 to 5'),

N('phare-cap-des-rosiers',"Cap-des-Rosiers Lighthouse",'Gaspé (Cap-des-Rosiers)','qc','light','standing',48.8563,-64.2011,'exact','open','walk','','1853–1858',
  "A tapering stone tower on cliffs at the point where the St. Lawrence meets the Gulf, designed by John Page of the Department of Public Works and built by Charles François-Xavier Baby in 1853 to 1858. At 34.1 m (112 ft) the Register calls it the tallest lighthouse in Canada. It has used its original optical apparatus since 1858 while the light itself went from oil to electricity and was fully automated in 1981.",
  "A white tower of marble stonework under a stucco coat, with small windows, a domed metal lantern on brackets, a spiral stair rising through nine storeys inside, and English firebrick lining; the original optics survive (Register). The Register says visitors are welcome.",
  "Canada's tallest lighthouse, a stone tower of the 1850s on the cliffs at the tip of the Gaspé.",
  "Cap-des-Rosiers lighthouse, on route 132 at Cap-des-Rosiers, run as a maritime historic site. No page of the operator was read, so its season and hours are not checked. Mapped lot on boulevard de Cap-des-Rosiers about 480 m south-west (OpenStreetMap); not checked whether there is closer parking.",
  [HP("Cap-des-Rosiers Lighthouse National Historic Site of Canada",7403),W('Cap-des-Rosiers Lighthouse'),W('List of historic places in Gaspésie-Îles-de-la-Madeleine')],
  th=[48.85457,-64.20704],thn="Lot on boulevard de Cap-des-Rosiers (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no',hours='?'),

N('banc-de-peche-paspebiac',"Banc-de-pêche de Paspébiac",'Paspébiac','qc','industry','standing',48.0212,-65.2548,'site','open','walk','','1783–1900',
  "A cod-fishery station on the sandspit round the Paspébiac barachois on the Baie des Chaleurs. Charles Robin and Co., founded in 1766, built the largest establishment here, and the Le Boutillier Brothers a smaller one from 1838; the fishery ran for more than 150 years. About 60 buildings once stood on the spit; a fire in 1964 destroyed most of them, leaving seven Robin buildings and a powder magazine and three Le Boutillier buildings.",
  "Ten wooden and other fishery buildings of the 1800s in a simple New England style on the west end of the spit, opposite the village, led by the Le Boutillier store, five storeys high (Register). The operator's page calls one of them among the largest fishery buildings ever built in America. Interior not checked.",
  "What the 1964 fire left of a great Jersey cod-fishing station on a Gaspé sandspit.",
  "Site historique national de Paspébiac, rue du Banc, Paspébiac, run as a museum. Season and hours not checked: the operator's page read gave none. Mapped lot on avenue du Quai about 90 m south (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Banc de Pêche de Paspébiac National Historic Site of Canada",12623),L('Site historique national de Paspébiac','https://sitepaspebiac.ca/'),W('List of historic places in Gaspésie-Îles-de-la-Madeleine')],
  th=[48.0204,-65.25498],thn="Lot on avenue du Quai (OpenStreetMap)",brick='?',hours='?'),

N('village-minier-bourlamaque',"Village minier de Bourlamaque",'Val-d\'Or','qc','town','standing',48.0954,-77.7711,'area','public','walk','','1935',
  "A company town laid out in 1935 for the Lamaque gold mine, one of the biggest gold producers in Quebec's history, during the Abitibi gold rush of the 1930s; Teck-Hughes Gold Mines had the plan drawn about 1934 (designer named in the Register: Matthew M. Dineen). It was classified a historic site in 1979.",
  "Some 22 hectares on two long east–west avenues and five cross streets, with 82 buildings: workers' houses of spruce logs built to four standard plans, boarding houses and a cafeteria, and on a wooded rise to the north the managers' three white-stuccoed houses (two Tudor style), the guest house and the hospital (Register). The Cité de l'Or says about 60 log houses are still lived in. Beside it the Cité de l'Or shows the old Lamaque mine's dry house and hoist room and runs underground tours in season.",
  "A 1935 gold-rush company town of spruce-log houses, still lived in.",
  "Streets of the City of Val-d'Or; the houses are private homes, so look from the street. The Cité de l'Or (90 avenue Perrault) says the self-guided tour of the village is free and possible all year; its mine tours and museum are seasonal (its schedule page, read November 2026: museum open to early October 2026). Mapped lot on avenue Perreault about 330 m east (OpenStreetMap). The pin is the Register point for the whole village.",
  [HP("Site historique du Village-Minier-de-Bourlamaque",9110),L('La Cité de l\'Or: schedule','https://www.citedelor.com/en/page/index.cfm?PageID=21'),L('La Cité de l\'Or: mining village','https://www.citedelor.com/en/page/index.cfm?PageID=13'),W('List of historic places in Abitibi-Témiscamingue')],
  th=[48.09451,-77.76682],thn="Lot on avenue Perreault (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no',hours='Village streets open all year'),

N('fort-temiscamingue',"Obadjiwan–Fort Témiscamingue",'Duhamel-Ouest','qc','fort','traces',47.28861,-79.46139,'area','open','walk','','1720–1902',
  "A fur-trade post at the narrows of Lake Timiskaming, on an Algonquin camping ground called Obadjiwan, on the canoe route between Hudson Bay and Montreal. French merchants founded it in 1720 (an earlier post of about 1679 across the lake was destroyed in 1688); the North West Company and then the Hudson's Bay Company ran it, and it became a general store before closing in 1902. Parks Canada now runs it with the Timiskaming First Nation (Wikipedia).",
  "Little of the fort remains: Wikipedia says about 1980 only some chimneys stood, and shows old graves on the site. A visitor centre and exhibits explain it, and the park's woods include a stand of twisted cedars called the Enchanted Forest (Wikipedia). What survives on the ground now was not checked.",
  "A fur-trade post of 1720 at the narrows of Lake Timiskaming, now marked by chimneys and graves.",
  "Parks Canada national historic site near Ville-Marie, in Duhamel-Ouest. The visitor centre is open in summer (Parks Canada hours page, read November 2026). Mapped lot on the Sentier Le Marais about 250 m north-east (OpenStreetMap). The pin is the Wikipedia coordinate for the site, not a structure.",
  [W('Fort Témiscamingue'),PC('Obadjiwan–Fort Témiscamingue hours','obadjiwan-temiscamingue/visit/heures-hours')],
  th=[47.28959,-79.45838],thn="Lot by the Sentier Le Marais (OpenStreetMap)",brick='?',
  hours='June 20 to September 7, 2026: daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.',conf='mixed'),

N('phare-pointe-des-monts',"Phare de Pointe-des-Monts",'Baie-Trinité','qc','light','standing',49.3256,-67.3667,'exact','open','drive','','1829–1964',
  "The second-oldest lighthouse on the St. Lawrence, built in 1829 and 1830 by Trinity House of Quebec where the river narrows dangerously on the north shore; the first was on Île Verte (1806–1809). A store for shipwrecked sailors was added in 1831 and replaced in 1850 by a shelter joined to the tower, a fog cannon came in 1867, and the light was replaced by a new one in 1964. It was classified in 1965.",
  "A seven-storey stone tower over 21 m high, sheathed in cedar shingles, white with two red bands, under an octagonal metal lantern, on a rocky islet cut off at high tide. Beside it the wooden keeper's house of 1911–1912, a brick powder magazine of 1867, a henhouse and a shed, all in white and red (Register). The operator's page shows an exhibition inside and rooms to stay in the keeper's house.",
  "The second-oldest lighthouse on the St. Lawrence, a red-banded tower on a tidal islet.",
  "Run by a non-profit as a museum and inn, 1830 chemin du Vieux-Phare, Pointe-des-Monts (Baie-Trinité). The operator's page read gave no current season or hours (hours not checked). No parking is mapped; the pin check shows the road end. The islet is cut off at high tide (Register).",
  [HP("Phare de Pointe-des-Monts",9366),L('Phare de Pointe-des-Monts','https://pharedepointedesmonts.com/'),W('List of historic places in Côte-Nord')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),

N('grange-ronde-mansonville',"Grange ronde de Mansonville",'Potton (Mansonville)','qc','landmark','standing',45.05306,-72.39383,'exact','view','walk','','1911',
  "A round dairy barn built in 1911 for Robert J. Jersey (the Register's date; a tourism listing gives 1912 and Robert E. Jersey). Only a few dozen round barns were built in Quebec, between 1885 and 1920, most of them in the Eastern Townships near the American border, where seven survive. It is the only one left of four in the township of Potton, was farmed until 1990, and is one of very few in a village centre. Cited a historic monument by the township.",
  "A three-level circular barn of horizontal boards under a round gambrel roof with a lantern at the top and three gabled dormers, with a covered ramp (garnaud) up to the top floor (Register). Interior not checked.",
  "One of seven round barns left in the Eastern Townships, standing in the middle of a village.",
  "Owned by the Township of Potton, on rue Joseph-Blanchet set back from the street in Mansonville. No page of the township on visiting it was read: look from outside (hours not checked). Mapped lot on route de Mansonville about 260 m north (OpenStreetMap).",
  [HP("Grange ronde de Mansonville",16207),W('List of historic places in Estrie')],
  th=[45.05526,-72.3926],thn="Lot on route de Mansonville (OpenStreetMap)",brick='no',conf='mixed'),

N('moulin-banal-des-aulnaies',"Moulin banal des Aulnaies",'Saint-Roch-des-Aulnaies','qc','industry','standing',47.3156,-70.1438,'site','open','drive','','1842',
  "A stone water mill built in 1842 by the merchant Amable Dionne, seigneur of Les Aulnaies from 1837, on the site of two earlier mills of 1738 and 1789, on the Rivière Ferrée (Le Bras). It was the seigneury's banal mill, where tenants had to grind their grain and pay a share. Dionne fitted it from the start to saw wood as well. In the 1980s it was set up with the nearby seigneurial manor as an interpretation centre on the seigneurial system.",
  "A three-and-a-half-storey stone mill on a rectangular plan under a plain gable roof, built against the slope down to the river, at the corner of route de la Seigneurie and route 132. It was built with four sets of millstones, each floor with its own task: the power drive at the bottom, the stones and the bolter above, the grain cleaner and the counter at the top (Register).",
  "The 1842 seigneurial mill of a Côte-du-Sud seigneury, shown with the manor house beside it.",
  "Part of La Seigneurie des Aulnaies, 525 route de la Seigneurie, with the classified manor next door. Its own website could not be read when this was written (hours not checked). Parking not checked: the only mapped lot nearby is a highway rest area.",
  [HP("Moulin banal des Aulnaies",4451),W('List of historic places in Chaudière-Appalaches')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),

N('grosse-ile-irish-memorial',"Grosse-Île quarantine station and Irish Memorial",'Saint-Antoine-de-l\'Isle-aux-Grues (Grosse-Île)','qc','inst','standing',47.0246,-70.6713,'area','boat','boat','','1832–1937',
  "An island in the St. Lawrence below Quebec City that was the quarantine station for the port of Quebec from 1832 to 1937, when that port was the main way into Canada for immigrants. Thousands of Irish immigrants died here, above all in the typhus epidemic of 1847; a Celtic cross was put up in 1909 in their memory. Dr. Frederick Montizambert, superintendent for the last 30 years of the 1800s, made it a model for a new generation of Canadian quarantine stations.",
  "Along the south shore of the island, in three sectors joined by a road: plain wooden buildings and two of brick from 1832 to 1937, among them the disinfection building, hotels, wash house, bakery, sailors' quarters, Anglican and Catholic chapels, the vaccination office and the guardhouse; the Irish cemetery of unmarked grassed graves with the doctors' monument; the Celtic cross; and the west wharf with remains of the 1846–1847 one (Register).",
  "The island where the immigrants of 1847 were held, and where thousands of Irish lie in unmarked graves.",
  "Parks Canada national historic site, reached only by the Croisières Lachance boat from Berthier-sur-Mer or by air from Montmagny (Parks Canada). Visit times depend on the carrier's trips; ask the carrier for departures. The pin marks the island's built-up south shore in general.",
  [HP("Grosse Île and the Irish Memorial National Historic Site of Canada",1189),PC('Grosse-Île','grosseile'),PC('Grosse-Île hours','grosseile/visit/heures-hours'),W('List of historic places in Chaudière-Appalaches')],
  brick='some',hours='2026: June 3 to 18 and September 8 to October 10 Wednesday to Sunday; June 19 to September 7 daily; times set by the boat'),
]

